          'Set the script side: a copyist producing a few pages a day needs well over a year for 1,282 pages — say one and a half scribe-years per copy, times 180 copies.',
          'Compare: about 270 scribe-years against roughly thirty press worker-years — an order of magnitude, with each printed copy moreover identical to the others.',
          'State the result: the press competes on the F/n curve rather than on the speed of any single page, and this arithmetic, more than any single invention, is what swept Europe.',
